follow-up at once.</p>\n<h4 id=\"possibilities-and-tradeoffs\">Possibilities and tradeoffs</h4>\n<h5 id=\"1-historical-starting-conditions-open-subsequent-history\">1. Historical starting conditions, open subsequent history</h5>\n<p><strong>PROPOSED:</strong> Reproduce institutions, material constraints and initial pressures, then let people generate history. Strong agency; exact familiar events cannot be guaranteed.</p>\n<h5 id=\"2-selected-regional-reconstructions-in-a-continuous-world\">2. Selected regional reconstructions in a continuous world</h5>\n<p><strong>PROPOSED:</strong> Different zones test different periods while sharing real logistics and consequences. Rich environment ladder; transitions and knowledge leakage need credible boundaries.</p>\n<h5 id=\"3-a-single-society-with-accelerated-surface-development\">3. A single society with accelerated surface development</h5>\n<p><strong>PROPOSED:</strong> Supports Great War through startup-era change and inherited records. Strong causal continuity; acceleration must not make families or technology arbitrary.</p>\n<h5 id=\"4-historical-constraints-beneath-an-explicit-safety-substrate\">4. Historical constraints beneath an explicit safety substrate</h5>\n<p><strong>PROPOSED:</strong> Advanced infrastructure preserves bodies and basic rights while limiting visible affordances. Helps legitimacy; the story must show costly permitted harm without claiming catastrophe was harmless.</p>\n<h4 id=\"targeted-follow-up-questions\">Targeted follow-up questions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Who is native to the reconstructed society, and can they decline its continuation?</li>\n<li>Which scarcity is physical and which administratively imposed?</li>\n<li>Which historical cruelty must never be reenacted merely for fidelity?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"scene-test\">Scene test</h4>\n<p><strong>Non-canon situation:</strong> A seemingly mundane failure exposes a hidden grid intervention, saving a person while contradicting the era’s official account.</p>\n<p>Record what each person wants, can know, can refuse, and can actually change. Name the observable difference between the selected alternatives.</p>\n<h4 id=\"adversarial-questions\">Adversarial questions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>If leaders can manufacture abundance and prevent injury, why preserve this specific deprivation or danger?</li>\n<li>Which established constraint would this answer break, and what evidence would reveal that break before the payoff?</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"completion-checklist\">Completion checklist</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>☑ Answer the central question in the author&#x27;s own words.</li>\n<li>☑ Identify the chosen possibility, a modified combination, or a different answer; explain rejected tradeoffs.</li>\n<li>☑ Resolve prerequisites 01 and 02.</li>\n<li>☑ Run the scene test above and state the consequence for the people involved.</li>\n<li>☑ Answer the adversarial challenge without granting unexplained knowledge or authority.</li>\n<li>☑ Record what remains working, proposed, or unresolved.</li>\n<li>☑ Obtain explicit author acceptance before updating canon or workflow progress.</li>\n<li>☑ Trace the accepted answer through the affected notes and rebuild both projections.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"answer-and-decision-record\">Answer and decision record</h4>\n<pre><code>Module: 04 · Physical historical reconstruction\nGate: What does reconstruction reproduce faithfully, and what is allowed or required to change?\nState: AUTHOR-ACCEPTED\nAuthor answer (verbatim): preserved in the linked session record\nExact restatement: accepted result above\nSelected option or new answer: historical anchors and public roles customized for the effectiveness of the colonization process\nWhy this tradeoff: real historical information supplies pressure and meaning while adaptation supports training, containment, bonding and sustainability\nRejected alternatives and reasons: literal replay would erase the process&#x27;s ability to adapt and the participants&#x27; ability to