      "path": "07 Coordination/Story Completion Workflow/Workshop/14 - George’s necessary role.md",
      "markdown": "---\ntype: workshop-module\nstatus: proposed-options-author-gate-open\nmodule: 14\ntitle: George’s necessary role\ngate: What can George’s decisions reveal or change that neither Konrad nor a generic proxy can?\nprerequisites: 12, 13, 15\nupdated: 2026-09-05\n---\n\n# 14 · George’s necessary role\n\n## Purpose\n\nResolve overlap with Konrad by assigning George a distinct causal and emotional function.\n\nThis packet supports the existing Story Completion Workflow. It does not advance a checklist or settle canon. All alternatives below are PROPOSED; the gate is OPEN. Central synthesis: Astra, reviewed for integration on September 5.\n\n## Relevant source notes\n\n- [[02 Story/Characters/George White - A Life Beneath the Ice]]\n- [[02 Story/Characters/George White - The Collapse of the Lie]]\n- [[02 Story/Systems/Competitive Environments - Control Inversion and Sylvan Endgame]]\n\n## Confirmed facts and current working constraints\n\n- George is weaponized and intended to absorb his father’s defeat.\n- The new Samuel–Konrad frame controls Book One; George remains subordinate pending reconciliation.\n\nThese constraints inherit their source status. A working physical model, timing estimate, or accepted macro constraint does not establish every implementation detail.\n\n## Unresolved gaps and contradictions\n\n- Timing; active command channel; his choice; final responsibility; duplication of isolation.\n\n## Prerequisite decisions\n\nModules: 12, 13, 15. Read their accepted results before closing this gate. Open prerequisites can be explored provisionally, but do not mark dependent choices final. Module numbers are topic IDs; use the dependency order in the workshop index.\n\n## Central author gate\n\n> What can George’s decisions reveal or change that neither Konrad nor a generic proxy can?\n\nAsk this question alone during actual use. Do not ask every follow-up at once.\n\n## Possibilities and tradeoffs\n\n### 1. Active command witness who can refuse the final order\n\n**PROPOSED:** His firsthand connection to Samuel exposes live maintenance. Strong present agency; corroboration is needed so one testimony does not solve everything.\n\n### 2. Scapegoat whose protection tests Sylvan’s restraint\n\n**PROPOSED:** Samuel tries to make Sylvan dispose of him. Gives Sylvan a costly human choice; avoid making George entirely Sylvan’s dependent or clearing his conduct.\n\n### 3. Earlier-model recovery counterexample\n\n**PROPOSED:** George and his older bond confront corrupted premises, showing old technology need not remain evil. Strengthens moral/technical distinction; exact recovery cannot be effortless.\n\n### 4. Parallel partial awakening in a limited interlude\n\n**PROPOSED:** George’s restricted viewpoint makes Samuel’s servicing of reality visible while Konrad stays isolated. Economical suspense; risks another exposition thread unless George acts on what he learns.\n\n## Targeted follow-up questions\n\n- What evidence can only George supply, or what decision can only he make?\n- What does refusal cost him?\n- Which crimes remain his after command responsibility is proven?\n\n## Scene test\n\n**Non-canon situation:** George receives an instruction justified by a fresh sign; instead of executing it, he preserves the original request and asks a question Samuel cannot answer consistently.\n\nRecord what each person wants, can know, can refuse, and can actually change. Name the observable difference between the selected alternatives.\n\n## Adversarial questions\n\n- If George were removed, would Book One’s plot or moral result change? If only exposition disappears, his role is not yet necessary.\n- Which established constraint would this answer break, and what evidence would reveal that break before the payoff?\n\n## Completion checklist\n\n- [ ] Answer the central question in the author's own words.\n- [ ] Identify the chosen possibility, a modified combination, or a different answer; explain rejected tradeoffs.\n- [ ] Resolve or explicitly defer prerequisites 12, 13, 15.\n- [ ] Run the scene test above and state the consequence for the people involved.\n- [ ] Answer the adversarial challenge without granting unexplained knowledge or authority.\n- [ ] Record what remains working, proposed, or unresolved.\n- [ ] Obtain explicit author acceptance before updating canon or workflow progress.\n- [ ] Trace the accepted answer through the affected notes and rebuild both projections.\n\n## Answer and decision record\n\n```text\nModule: 14 · George’s necessary role\nGate: What can George’s decisions reveal or change that neither Konrad nor a generic proxy can?\nState: DRAFT / PROPOSED / AUTHOR-ACCEPTED / REJECTED / DEFERRED\nAuthor answer (verbatim):\nExact restatement:\nSelected option or new answer:\nWhy this tradeoff:\nRejected alternatives and reasons:\nPrerequisites resolved / deferred:\nScene test: choices, permissions, evidence, cost:\nAdversarial result:\nRemaining uncertainty:\nExplicit acceptance wording and date:\nAffected notes updated:\nQA decision reference:\nWorkflow task and macro-depth effect:\nWebsite rebuild and verification:\n```\n\n## Notes and website sections affected\n\n- [[02 Story/Characters/George White - A Life Beneath the Ice]]\n- [[02 Story/Characters/George White - The Collapse of the Lie]]\n- [[02 Story/Systems/Competitive Environments - Control Inversion and Sylvan Endgame]]\n\n- Public atlas: `docs/characters.html`, the matching source in `05 Public/Atlas/`, and the workshop projection.\n- Development Explorer: the relevant topic view, this source packet, and linked evidence/decision records.\n- [[07 QA/Decisions]], [[07 QA/Questions]], and [[07 Coordination/Weekly Synthesis/CURRENT-WEEK-INTAKE]]. Update [[07 Coordination/CURRENT-PICKUP]] and the current task only if the author accepts a workflow-relevant decision.\n",
