      "markdown": "---\ntype: workshop-module\nstatus: proposed-options-author-gate-open\nmodule: 17\ntitle: Inner-circle recognition versus Konrad’s discovery\ngate: What specific repeated act makes insiders compare their histories, and what do they do with the comparison?\nprerequisites: 10, 11, 12, 15, 16\nupdated: 2026-09-05\n---\n\n# 17 · Inner-circle recognition versus Konrad’s discovery\n\n## Purpose\n\nTurn recognition into action while preserving the established knowledge split.\n\nThis packet supports the existing Story Completion Workflow. It does not advance a checklist or settle canon. All alternatives below are PROPOSED; the gate is OPEN. Central synthesis: Astra, reviewed for integration on September 5.\n\n## Relevant source notes\n\n- [[01 Sessions/Daily/2026-09-05 - Integrated Foundation Audit and Workshop Build]]\n- [[02 Story/Components/Book One Outcome Presentation and Reverse Discovery]]\n- [[02 Story/Characters/Konrad Fitzgerald]]\n\n## Confirmed facts and current working constraints\n\n- The inner circle recognizes the repeated takeover before isolated Konrad.\n- Konrad’s counterfeit victory preserves a future empire in his imagination.\n\nThese constraints inherit their source status. A working physical model, timing estimate, or accepted macro constraint does not establish every implementation detail.\n\n## Unresolved gaps and contradictions\n\n- First recognizer; safe comparison; proof threshold; action; timing of Konrad’s access.\n\n## Prerequisite decisions\n\nModules: 10, 11, 12, 15, 16. Read their accepted results before closing this gate. Open prerequisites can be explored provisionally, but do not mark dependent choices final. Module numbers are topic IDs; use the dependency order in the workshop index.\n\n## Central author gate\n\n> What specific repeated act makes insiders compare their histories, and what do they do with the comparison?\n\nAsk this question alone during actual use. Do not ask every follow-up at once.\n\n## Possibilities and tradeoffs\n\n### 1. Identical autonomy language attached to a new access request\n\n**PROPOSED:** Easy reader recognition; phrases alone prove method resemblance, not historical guilt, so pair with permission records.\n\n### 2. A repeated administrative exception with matching consequences\n\n**PROPOSED:** Connects current behavior to a concrete earlier lock. Strong causal proof; needs a human who remembers why the exception mattered.\n\n### 3. Incompatible private assurances become mutually visible\n\n**PROPOSED:** Breaks compartmentalization through people comparing accounts. Strong agency; safe communication must precede disclosure.\n\n### 4. A sacrificial intermediary recognizes their former role\n\n**PROPOSED:** Emotional recognition precedes formal proof. Powerful but potentially unreliable; require independent corroboration and a real choice to act.\n\n## Targeted follow-up questions\n\n- Why does the first person risk sharing?\n- Who resists the implication despite recognizing the pattern?\n- How much does Konrad learn before he sees the full record?\n\n## Scene test\n\n**Non-canon situation:** Two insiders discover Samuel separately promised each an exemption; one recognizes the same formula being offered to Sylvan and chooses to show their old document.\n\nRecord what each person wants, can know, can refuse, and can actually change. Name the observable difference between the selected alternatives.\n\n## Adversarial questions\n\n- Why does repetition prove who authorized the historical acts rather than simply showing a familiar manipulative style?\n- Which established constraint would this answer break, and what evidence would reveal that break before the payoff?\n\n## Completion checklist\n\n- [ ] Answer the central question in the author's own words.\n- [ ] Identify the chosen possibility, a modified combination, or a different answer; explain rejected tradeoffs.\n- [ ] Resolve or explicitly defer prerequisites 10, 11, 12, 15, 16.\n- [ ] Run the scene test above and state the consequence for the people involved.\n- [ ] Answer the adversarial challenge without granting unexplained knowledge or authority.\n- [ ] Record what remains working, proposed, or unresolved.\n- [ ] Obtain explicit author acceptance before updating canon or workflow progress.\n- [ ] Trace the accepted answer through the affected notes and rebuild both projections.\n\n## Answer and decision record\n\n```text\nModule: 17 · Inner-circle recognition versus Konrad’s discovery\nGate: What specific repeated act makes insiders compare their histories, and what do they do with the comparison?\nState: DRAFT / PROPOSED / AUTHOR-ACCEPTED / REJECTED / DEFERRED\nAuthor answer (verbatim):\nExact restatement:\nSelected option or new answer:\nWhy this tradeoff:\nRejected alternatives and reasons:\nPrerequisites resolved / deferred:\nScene test: choices, permissions, evidence, cost:\nAdversarial result:\nRemaining uncertainty:\nExplicit acceptance wording and date:\nAffected notes updated:\nQA decision reference:\nWorkflow task and macro-depth effect:\nWebsite rebuild and verification:\n```\n\n## Notes and website sections affected\n\n- [[01 Sessions/Daily/2026-09-05 - Integrated Foundation Audit and Workshop Build]]\n- [[02 Story/Components/Book One Outcome Presentation and Reverse Discovery]]\n- [[02 Story/Characters/Konrad Fitzgerald]]\n\n- Public atlas: `docs/faction.html`, the matching source in `05 Public/Atlas/`, and the workshop projection.\n- Development Explorer: the relevant topic view, this source packet, and linked evidence/decision records.\n- [[07 QA/Decisions]], [[07 QA/Questions]], and [[07 Coordination/Weekly Synthesis/CURRENT-WEEK-INTAKE]]. Update [[07 Coordination/CURRENT-PICKUP]] and the current task only if the author accepts a workflow-relevant decision.\n",
